<tt xmlns="http://www.w3.org/ns/ttml" xmlns:tts="http://www.w3.org/ns/ttml#styling" xml:lang="en"><head><layout xmlns:tts="http://www.w3.org/ns/ttml#styling"><region xml:id="r1"><style tts:origin="0% 85%"/><style tts:extent="100% 15%"/><style tts:opacity=".85"/><style tts:fontSize="18px"/><style tts:backgroundColor="black"/><style tts:color="#FFD056"/><style tts:textAlign="center"/><style tts:displayAlign="after"/><style tts:padding="10% 5%"/><style tts:wrapOption="wrap"/></region></layout><styling xmlns:tts="http://www.w3.org/ns/ttml#styling"><style id="s0" tts:color="#FFD056" tts:fontSize="95%" tts:textAlign="center" tts:wrapOption="wrap%"/></styling></head><body><div style="s0"><p begin="00:00:02.4" end="00:00:03.6">Hello everybody.</p><p begin="00:00:03.6" end="00:00:05.1">My name is Sylvia marshall.</p><p begin="00:00:05.1" end="00:00:07.5">I'm one of the academic medicine scholars.</p><p begin="00:00:07.5" end="00:00:11.9">And today we're going to be discussing<br/>arterial venus and length attic disease.</p><p begin="00:00:14.4" end="00:00:18.4">Here are some of the session objectives<br/>go ahead and read them on your own time.</p><p begin="00:00:21.1" end="00:00:24.8">So when you have a vascular patient,<br/>just like any other patient,</p><p begin="00:00:24.8" end="00:00:29.5">It's really important to get<br/>a really good, detailed history and</p><p begin="00:00:29.5" end="00:00:35.6">specific make sure you understand the,<br/>the clinical history of their disease.</p><p begin="00:00:35.6" end="00:00:37.6">When did it start?</p><p begin="00:00:37.6" end="00:00:39.9">What kind of symptoms do they have?</p><p begin="00:00:39.9" end="00:00:44.8">These really clue you in to how far<br/>along they are in their disease process.</p><p begin="00:00:44.8" end="00:00:48.2">And that's important for you because that<br/>can really affect clinical management.</p><p begin="00:00:49.2" end="00:00:52.5">And it's, and of course important<br/>to get a detailed medical history</p><p begin="00:00:52.5" end="00:00:56.7">just because there are so many risk<br/>factors for these types of diseases.</p><p begin="00:00:58.2" end="00:01:00.9">Then, of course, also a smoking history.</p><p begin="00:01:00.9" end="00:01:05.4">As you'll see that that plays a big<br/>part in peripheral arterial disease.</p><p begin="00:01:07.3" end="00:01:09.5">So when it comes to the physical exam,</p><p begin="00:01:09.5" end="00:01:13.8">it's really important that you<br/>do a meticulous inspection.</p><p begin="00:01:13.8" end="00:01:17.8">So a lot of these do these processes,<br/>whether it's arterial venus or</p><p begin="00:01:17.8" end="00:01:20.7">lymphatic, they can all<br/>present with skin changes and</p><p begin="00:01:20.7" end="00:01:22.1">they can present with<br/>different skin changes.</p><p begin="00:01:22.1" end="00:01:26.6">So it's important that you get<br/>a good luck because I conclude un,</p><p begin="00:01:26.6" end="00:01:28">into what's going on.</p><p begin="00:01:29.7" end="00:01:33.6">Especially when it comes<br/>to diabetic patients,</p><p begin="00:01:33.6" end="00:01:35.4">it's important that you<br/>take their socks off.</p><p begin="00:01:35.4" end="00:01:38.1">So you can look at their toes because<br/>you'll see that especially arterial</p><p begin="00:01:38.1" end="00:01:42.7">problems, they affect the distal<br/>extremities, the toes, so</p><p begin="00:01:42.7" end="00:01:44.9">you don't want to Miss them.</p><p begin="00:01:44.9" end="00:01:49.2">Take pictures, pictures are really<br/>good way to, to monitor wound care.</p><p begin="00:01:50.3" end="00:01:53.8">You also want to how they,<br/>there pulses to they have bounding pulses.</p><p begin="00:01:53.8" end="00:01:56.6">Do they have no pulses you<br/>want to ask will take?</p><p begin="00:01:56.6" end="00:01:59.4">Because just by listening you can<br/>get a sense of where there's any</p><p begin="00:01:59.4" end="00:02:04.1">type of turbulence, any type of to know<br/>says or something along those lines.</p><p begin="00:02:05.9" end="00:02:09.4">So these are just some pictures of what<br/>you might fill in clinical practice.</p><p begin="00:02:09.4" end="00:02:13.5">The top pictures are more<br/>of the venus like picture.</p><p begin="00:02:13.5" end="00:02:17.9">So you'll see it's a Spectrum and<br/>it can range anywhere from varicose veins.</p><p begin="00:02:17.9" end="00:02:20.9">redeem minor skin changes to all sirs.</p><p begin="00:02:22" end="00:02:25.2">On the bottom,<br/>it's more of an arterial like picture, and</p><p begin="00:02:25.2" end="00:02:30.8">that's when you'll see more<br/>of a gang green all or type</p><p begin="00:02:33.4" end="00:02:38.1">So with a lot of these patients, whether<br/>they have arterial or venus issues,</p><p begin="00:02:38.1" end="00:02:42.4">they will come in often complaining<br/>of a non healing wound.</p><p begin="00:02:42.4" end="00:02:46.9">And that is a big indication that they<br/>have some type of vascular issue.</p><p begin="00:02:49.7" end="00:02:54.6">So this slide just goes over where<br/>to find the different arteries,</p><p begin="00:02:54.6" end="00:02:57.4">femoral property, or post irritable.</p><p begin="00:02:57.4" end="00:02:58.7">And that are cells pitas.</p><p begin="00:02:58.7" end="00:03:03.5">These are the pulses that you can<br/>feel that's apply the lower lim, so</p><p begin="00:03:03.5" end="00:03:06.9">I'm not going to go into<br/>how you find them all.</p><p begin="00:03:06.9" end="00:03:10.7">But I just want to emphasize that it's<br/>important to always compare both sides.</p><p begin="00:03:10.7" end="00:03:13.9">So we know that in normal<br/>pulse is a 2 plus, so</p><p begin="00:03:13.9" end="00:03:18.1">check all these, all these pulses<br/>bilaterally and document them.</p><p begin="00:03:21.8" end="00:03:24.4">So this is the outline of what<br/>we're going to be discussing today.</p><p begin="00:03:24.4" end="00:03:27.7">We're not going to be discussing the a<br/>order because that's going to be covered</p><p begin="00:03:27.7" end="00:03:31.9">in another lecture, nor we're going to<br/>be discussing the visceral arteries.</p><p begin="00:03:31.9" end="00:03:35.8">We're really going to be holding<br/>in on the peripheral arteries and</p><p begin="00:03:35.8" end="00:03:37.3">also those supplying the brain.</p><p begin="00:03:39.1" end="00:03:42.1">So 1st we're going to<br/>discuss per arterial disease.</p><p begin="00:03:42.1" end="00:03:45.6">This can be called different things<br/>like peripheral artery, disease,</p><p begin="00:03:45.6" end="00:03:49.5">peripheral vascular disease,<br/>they all mean the same thing.</p><p begin="00:03:49.5" end="00:03:52.1">So what happens is that<br/>you get an obstruction or</p><p begin="00:03:52.1" end="00:03:58.2">deterioration of the arteries other than<br/>know, supplying the heart or the brain.</p><p begin="00:03:58.2" end="00:04:00.1">Because we know if they were to<br/>supply the heart of the brain,</p><p begin="00:04:00.1" end="00:04:05.2">that would either cause a heart attack or<br/>stroke.</p><p begin="00:04:05.2" end="00:04:09.2">So there's normally happens<br/>in the lower extremity,</p><p begin="00:04:09.2" end="00:04:11">it can happen in the upper of somebody.</p><p begin="00:04:11" end="00:04:16.3">It's just not as common in the major<br/>pathophysiology, process, atherosclerosis.</p><p begin="00:04:16.3" end="00:04:21.9">So what happens here is you get<br/>this atherosclerotic build up</p><p begin="00:04:21.9" end="00:04:28">in the vessels and this grows there<br/>are symptoms worse and because</p><p begin="00:04:28" end="00:04:31.5">they're going to get diminished blood<br/>flow that's just worsening over time.</p><p begin="00:04:32.5" end="00:04:38.5">And so you could imagine that if you have<br/>a big auto score roddic plaque in say,</p><p begin="00:04:38.5" end="00:04:43.5">the pop, little artery which supplies the<br/>lower limb, you could imagine that as you</p><p begin="00:04:43.5" end="00:04:49.7">walk and as you're increasing oxygen<br/>demand in say to cabs, your blood,</p><p begin="00:04:49.7" end="00:04:55.2">these vessels are not able to supply<br/>adequate oxygen to those tissues.</p><p begin="00:04:55.2" end="00:04:57.1">So that's going to present<br/>as pain discomfort</p><p begin="00:04:57.1" end="00:05:02.3">that will be relieved once<br/>that oxygen demand decreases.</p><p begin="00:05:02.3" end="00:05:07.3">So you're going to see these patients<br/>present with what's called clot occasion.</p><p begin="00:05:07.3" end="00:05:08.6">convocation.</p><p begin="00:05:08.6" end="00:05:11.6">This is a triad and definitely know this.</p><p begin="00:05:11.6" end="00:05:15.8">It's very important, so<br/>it is pain with activity</p><p begin="00:05:15.8" end="00:05:20.5">that's relieved by rest and reproducible<br/>with the same level of activity.</p><p begin="00:05:20.5" end="00:05:25.4">So you'll see this patient that will say<br/>I used to be able to walk 10 blocks.</p><p begin="00:05:25.4" end="00:05:30.6">And now after 5, I have to take,<br/>I have to take a break and</p><p begin="00:05:30.6" end="00:05:34.5">you know, living in the city, this is<br/>something that a lot of people will say.</p><p begin="00:05:34.5" end="00:05:39.2">So this will clue you in that<br/>is per arterial disease and</p><p begin="00:05:39.2" end="00:05:41.4">that they need further work up.</p><p begin="00:05:41.4" end="00:05:45.9">And so since this is associated<br/>with atherosclerosis,</p><p begin="00:05:45.9" end="00:05:50.1">you'll see these have the same risk<br/>factors as say an M I or a stroke.</p><p begin="00:05:50.1" end="00:05:54.6">So obesity, smoking, diabetes,</p><p begin="00:05:54.6" end="00:05:58.7">hypertension, those are all risk factors<br/>for peripheral arterial disease.</p><p begin="00:06:01.7" end="00:06:05.5">This is also really important to<br/>distinguish between pseudo qualification.</p><p begin="00:06:05.5" end="00:06:08.1">So this is normally<br/>caused by spinal notice.</p><p begin="00:06:08.1" end="00:06:14.8">So they can also get a pain in<br/>their legs like say when they walk.</p><p begin="00:06:14.8" end="00:06:19">But this is different because<br/>it is relieved by position.</p><p begin="00:06:19" end="00:06:22.9">So we know that finals gnosis, you'll<br/>see what's called a shopping cart sign,</p><p begin="00:06:22.9" end="00:06:27.8">where they hunch over, and that selection<br/>of the spine relieve their pain.</p><p begin="00:06:27.8" end="00:06:30.4">This is not something that<br/>you're going to see in,</p><p begin="00:06:30.4" end="00:06:33.7">in clarification from<br/>purple arterial disease.</p><p begin="00:06:33.7" end="00:06:38.2">It's not going to change bi position,<br/>it's going to change by resting.</p><p begin="00:06:38.2" end="00:06:42.4">So, also with this, you'll see they<br/>have variable walking distances.</p><p begin="00:06:42.4" end="00:06:46.5">So sometimes we'll say I can walk 10<br/>blocks until my until my legs are and</p><p begin="00:06:46.5" end="00:06:48.1">then sometimes it's 5.</p><p begin="00:06:48.1" end="00:06:52.7">So it changes because again,<br/>it has to do with there.</p><p begin="00:06:52.7" end="00:06:55.3">It has to do with<br/>an actual nerve impingement</p><p begin="00:06:55.3" end="00:06:56.9">rather than a lack of blood flow.</p><p begin="00:07:00.4" end="00:07:02.9">So in general symptoms of p, A,</p><p begin="00:07:02.9" end="00:07:07.2">D tend to occur one level below<br/>the level of obstruction.</p><p begin="00:07:07.2" end="00:07:13.8">So say for instance, somebody has<br/>a obstruction in the political arteries.</p><p begin="00:07:13.8" end="00:07:18.5">This will cause clot occasions and<br/>gums below that level.</p><p begin="00:07:18.5" end="00:07:21.9">So say for instance in the CAPS there's,<br/>of course, there's 100 percent.</p><p begin="00:07:21.9" end="00:07:26.1">There are no variations and<br/>people symptoms.</p><p begin="00:07:26.1" end="00:07:28.4">But in general,<br/>this is a good rule of thumb.</p><p begin="00:07:28.4" end="00:07:33.5">And again, clot occasion is pain with<br/>activity that's relieved by rest and</p><p begin="00:07:33.5" end="00:07:35.7">reproducible with the same<br/>level of activity.</p><p begin="00:07:39.1" end="00:07:40.9">And a good way to screen for ph.</p><p begin="00:07:40.9" end="00:07:45.1">D is by doing what's called an ache and<br/>angle break your index.</p><p begin="00:07:45.1" end="00:07:48.4">So what this is,<br/>is you take this installing pressure</p><p begin="00:07:48.4" end="00:07:53.1">of the break you'll artery and<br/>compare it to those of the angle arteries,</p><p begin="00:07:53.1" end="00:07:57.3">whether that is the post here to be on or<br/>the doors alice pitas.</p><p begin="00:07:57.3" end="00:08:03.5">So what you do, I think it's a lot easier<br/>if I just explain a doing actual math.</p><p begin="00:08:04.9" end="00:08:09">But so what you do is you take the break,</p><p begin="00:08:09" end="00:08:11.1">you'll artery of both the right and<br/>the left arm.</p><p begin="00:08:11.1" end="00:08:14.1">So 115100.</p><p begin="00:08:16.1" end="00:08:20.9">Then you take the 6 dollar pressure of<br/>both the doors, alice pitas in the post</p><p begin="00:08:20.9" end="00:08:26.8">error tibial, and you do the ratio of the<br/>highest number that you find at the angle.</p><p begin="00:08:26.8" end="00:08:32">So and again, you do this for both<br/>are right and the left leg separately.</p><p begin="00:08:32" end="00:08:37.9">So in this circumstance, say you have<br/>a 150, you break your solid pressure and</p><p begin="00:08:37.9" end="00:08:42.2">then the doors alice pitas is<br/>50 poster tibial is, is 25.</p><p begin="00:08:42.2" end="00:08:44.5">We're going to take the highest<br/>number of the 2, which is 50.</p><p begin="00:08:44.5" end="00:08:51.8">And you're going to take the ratio of<br/>that, so it's 50 divided by $150.00.</p><p begin="00:08:51.8" end="00:08:55.6">The angle over the break, your,<br/>I'm sorry, my handwriting is really bad.</p><p begin="00:08:55.6" end="00:08:57.9">I'm writing on a computer.</p><p begin="00:08:57.9" end="00:08:59.3">And then you do the same for the left.</p><p begin="00:08:59.3" end="00:09:04.8">So you do the highest of the 2<br/>witches 50 on the poster tibial and</p><p begin="00:09:04.8" end="00:09:07.3">again divide that by the break coil.</p><p begin="00:09:07.3" end="00:09:11.9">So 50 over 100.</p><p begin="00:09:11.9" end="00:09:17.8">So this is just a quick and<br/>easy way to screen somebody for p A D.</p><p begin="00:09:17.8" end="00:09:21.4">And So</p><p begin="00:09:21.4" end="00:09:26.8">by actually calculating that ratio,<br/>you're measuring the different or</p><p begin="00:09:26.8" end="00:09:32.1">the ratio of systolic pressure in<br/>the systemic system versus the ankle</p><p begin="00:09:32.1" end="00:09:37.3">in that just gives an indication of how<br/>severe the atherosclerotic plaque is.</p><p begin="00:09:37.3" end="00:09:38.2">For instance.</p><p begin="00:09:38.2" end="00:09:43.2">And how diminish the blood<br/>flow is to that artery.</p><p begin="00:09:43.2" end="00:09:48.3">So an A, B I of less than point<br/>9 is diagnostic for p A D.</p><p begin="00:09:48.3" end="00:09:50.2">And obviously it's a range.</p><p begin="00:09:50.2" end="00:09:56.6">So if you have an A,<br/>B I between 0.4 to 0.9.</p><p begin="00:09:56.6" end="00:10:02.5">That will present with qualification,<br/>leave it between 0 and 0.4.</p><p begin="00:10:02.5" end="00:10:08.1">These are the patients that tend to<br/>present with tissue loss or skin changes.</p><p begin="00:10:08.1" end="00:10:12.3">And if you have an A,<br/>B I between 0.2 and 0.4.</p><p begin="00:10:12.3" end="00:10:16.2">That'll present with rest pain or<br/>pain at rest.</p><p begin="00:10:17.6" end="00:10:20.3">So obviously the lower that the number is,</p><p be